From 1111111111111111111111111111111111111111 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Phillip Abbott <phillipa@vivi.io>
Date: Wed, 23 Jul 2026 15:30:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] gstamc: make MediaCodecInfo.isHardwareAccelerated() optional
 on Android < 10 (API < 29), deriving hardware-ness from codec names

Interim fix, pending upstream report.

isHardwareAccelerated() was added in API level 29 (Android 10). On Android
8/9 (API 26-28) the method does not exist, so gst_amc_jni_get_method_id()
throws NoSuchMethodError and gst_amc_codeclist_jni_static_init() returned
FALSE -- aborting MediaCodec codec-list initialisation entirely and leaving
gstamc with ZERO registered codecs (no hardware video decoders; all decode
falls back to software avdec_*).

Treat the lookup as optional: clear the exception, continue with a NULL
method id, and derive hardware-ness from the codec NAME at the call site,
using the same heuristic GStreamer 1.20's ranking used for years:
OMX.google.*, c2.android.* and *.sw.dec are Android's software codecs;
everything else (vendor OMX.* / c2.*) is assumed hardware.

v2 (2026-07-23): the original version of this patch assumed hardware = TRUE
for every codec when the API is unavailable. That is NOT safe: software and
hardware video decoders then tie at GST_RANK_PRIMARY + 1 and decodebin's
deterministic ordering (element factory name, alphabetical within equal
rank) picks the winner. On MT9950 (Android 9)
amcvideodec-omxgoogleh264decoder sorts before
amcvideodec-omxmtkvideodecoderavc, so the Google SOFTWARE decoder won -- and
its conservative DPB handling of H.264 streams lacking VUI
bitstream_restriction (e.g. macOS VideoToolbox output) adds a standing
~400ms output delay at 480p, making every frame arrive late at the sink and
be dropped (choppy ~7fps native share). Goldfinger (Amlogic) escaped only
because omxamlogicavcdecoderawesome happens to sort before
omxgoogleh264decoder. Deriving from names restores 1.20's selection
behaviour: vendor hardware decoders outrank Android's software codecs.
---
 .../sys/androidmedia/jni/gstamc-codeclist-jni.c    | 44 +++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/subprojects/gst-plugins-bad/sys/androidmedia/jni/gstamc-codeclist-jni.c b/subprojects/gst-plugins-bad/sys/androidmedia/jni/gstamc-codeclist-jni.c
index a18e0195fd..ea0e6ec586 100644
--- a/subprojects/gst-plugins-bad/sys/androidmedia/jni/gstamc-codeclist-jni.c
+++ b/subprojects/gst-plugins-bad/sys/androidmedia/jni/gstamc-codeclist-jni.c
@@ -163,10 +163,16 @@ gst_amc_codeclist_jni_static_init (void)
       gst_amc_jni_get_method_id (env, &err, media_codecinfo.klass,
       "isHardwareAccelerated", "()Z");
   if (!media_codecinfo.is_hardware_accelerated) {
-    GST_ERROR ("Failed to get android.media.MediaCodecInfo "
-        "isHardwareAccelerated(): %s", err->message);
+    /* isHardwareAccelerated() was added in API level 29 (Android 10). On
+     * Android 8/9 the method is absent and the lookup throws
+     * NoSuchMethodError, which aborted codec-list init and left gstamc with
+     * zero registered codecs (no hardware decoders). Treat it as optional;
+     * gst_amc_codec_info_handle_is_hardware_accelerated() derives the
+     * answer from the codec name when the method id is NULL. */
+    GST_WARNING ("android.media.MediaCodecInfo.isHardwareAccelerated() "
+        "unavailable (API < 29), will derive from codec names: %s",
+        err ? err->message : "no such method");
     g_clear_error (&err);
-    return FALSE;
   }
 
   media_codeccapabilities.klass =
@@ -329,6 +335,32 @@ gst_amc_codec_info_handle_is_hardware_accelerated (GstAmcCodecInfoHandle *
   g_return_val_if_fail (handle != NULL, FALSE);
   g_return_val_if_fail (is_hardware_accelerated != NULL, FALSE);
 
+  if (!media_codecinfo.is_hardware_accelerated) {
+    /* API < 29: isHardwareAccelerated() unavailable. Derive hardware-ness
+     * from the codec name, matching the heuristic GStreamer 1.20 used for
+     * ranking: OMX.google.*, c2.android.* and *.sw.dec are Android's
+     * software codecs; anything else (vendor OMX.* / c2.*) is assumed
+     * hardware. Blanket-TRUE is NOT safe here: it ties software and
+     * hardware decoders at the same rank, and element-factory name order
+     * then decides -- e.g. on MT9950 (Android 9) the Google SW H.264
+     * decoder beat the MTK hardware decoder, causing choppy 480p native
+     * share (deep DPB output delay on VUI-less streams). */
+    gchar *name = gst_amc_codec_info_handle_get_name (handle, err);
+
+    if (!name)
+      return FALSE;
+
+    *is_hardware_accelerated = !(g_str_has_prefix (name, "OMX.google.") ||
+        g_str_has_prefix (name, "c2.android.") ||
+        g_str_has_suffix (name, ".sw.dec"));
+
+    GST_INFO ("isHardwareAccelerated() unavailable (API < 29): derived %s "
+        "from codec name %s", *is_hardware_accelerated ? "HW" : "SW", name);
+
+    g_free (name);
+    return TRUE;
+  }
+
   env = gst_amc_jni_get_env ();
 
   if (!gst_amc_jni_call_boolean_method (env, err, handle->object,
-- 
2.52.0
